---
import { getImage, Image } from 'astro:assets';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import PhotoHero from '../components/PhotoHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Split from '../components/Split.astro';
import QuoteSection from '../components/QuoteSection.astro';
import { img } from '../lib/images';

const heroImage = img('home/story.jpg');
const ogImage = await getImage({ src: heroImage, width: 1200, format: 'webp' });

const houseImage = img('retreats/owners_house.jpg');
const terracesImage = img('farm/pergola.jpg');
const harmonyImage = img('story/roof_construction.jpg');
---

<PageLayout
  title="Our Story — Wild on the Farm | Boquete Cloud Forest Lodge"
  description="How a hotel and dive-shop owner from Roatán sold up for a mountainside in Boquete, and spent eighteen years building an organic farm before ever opening its doors to guests."
  path="/story/"
  image={ogImage.src}
  pullIntro="Wild on the Farm was born from a deep journey through Panama's wildest places. After years of exploration, the founder discovered this untouched land — and saw in it the perfect setting for a self-sustaining haven, where peace, nature and the art of living harmonise."
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <PhotoHero image={heroImage} alt="A living roof grown into the hillside at Wild on the Farm" eyebrow="Our History">
      Born from a journey through the wild.
    </PhotoHero>
  </Fragment>

  <Section label="A Journey Toward Harmony" heading="The Story Behind the Myth">
    <Split>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={harmonyImage}
          alt="Building the living roof at Wild on the Farm, high above the terraced hillside"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Born from a lost compass, built with passion and vision.</p>
        <p>
          Twenty years ago, I spent three years roaming the jungles and mountains of Panama. My
          daily life? A hammock, national parks, and the firm intention of losing myself on
          purpose. After roaming the wild corners of the globe on my own migratory legs, I finally
          arrived there — home…
        </p>
        <p>
          Coming out of the cloud forest, after a full week lost in La Amistad National Park, I
          stumbled upon this land, abandoned for eight years. Where others would have seen
          brambles, I saw my masterpiece: the perfect place to finally settle down and create a
          self-sufficient refuge in complete synergy with the wild.
        </p>
        <p>
          What fascinated me right away? The microclimate. An almost monastic calm, wrapped in
          jungle, that still offered a grandiose spectacle: a sweeping view over the Volcán Barú
          and the ceaseless ballet of the clouds. The land was untouched, and my mind was ready to
          experiment with a new way of living.
        </p>
        <p>
          I had time ahead of me, the funds I needed since I'd just sold my small hotel and dive
          shop in Roatán, and a wild urge to redefine the art of living at the summit.
        </p>
        <p>— The Founder, Wild on the Farm</p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Finding This Mountain" heading="The Art of Taming the Tropics">
    <Split>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={houseImage}
          alt="The original house on the property that would become Wild on the Farm"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Living in the tropics is a trade. And after a few decades of practice, I think I can
          say I've earned my diploma.
        </p>
        <p>
          My first lesson began in the seventies, in Africa. Three years among hunter-gatherers,
          wearing down my youth in traditional huts under a heat fierce enough to melt good sense
          itself. Ejected from that continent for excessive adaptation to primitivism, I found
          myself thrown back on the road, racing all the way from Chicoutimi in northern Canada to
          the far reaches of Patagonia. It was a Latin America living on borrowed time, torn apart
          by civil wars and dictatorships, still untouched by tourists but vibrant with its
          preserved traditions and Indigenous cultures.
        </p>
        <p>
          I spent years digging into every wound and every beauty of that continent. I lived on
          deserted beaches in Mexico; I shared the daily life of the Lacandon and the Shuar in the
          Ecuadorian Amazon. It was there, on the Río Napo in 1981, that I was arrested for
          espionage on the Peruvian border, caught in the middle of an absurd local war fought
          over a few drops of oil. Then came the years of art and craft, marked by forays onto the
          Peruvian altiplano while the Shining Path bled the country dry; I traveled then hidden
          beneath the tarps of cargo trucks to reach San Pedro de Cajas. I crossed Patagonia
          alone, loaded down with water, without a compass, and I collected my share of dangerous
          foolishness racing off to romantic rendezvous in Pinochet's Chile. Later, I settled by
          Lake Atitlán, captivated by the wonderful culture of the Kaqchikel people of Guatemala.
          I became passionate about their weavers and their huipiles, those traditional garments
          every community once wore with pride, before Western charity's cast-off clothing came to
          ruin that thousand-year-old craft.
        </p>
        <p>
          My world tour of the tropics taught me one fundamental truth: to truly appreciate them,
          you have to gain some elevation. The tropics only become perfect in the coolness of
          altitude. Today, faced with the cultural hegemony of a world grown too accessible and
          deadly dull, I chose to retreat into a fold of the earth that's difficult to reach. The
          bumpy track that leads up here may look like a Disney World attraction, but it's the
          only road to isolation, to putting a lifetime of gathered knowledge to use, and to
          generating a little harmony at the culmination of an unchained existence…
        </p>
        <p>Welcome to my ultimate paradise.</p>
        <p>— The Founder, Wild on the Farm</p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Built Slowly, By Choice" heading="A Manifesto for Slow Time">
    <Split reverse>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={terracesImage}
          alt="A pergola on the terraced farm property at Wild on the Farm"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          What followed wasn't a renovation. It was the birth of a landscape, shaped one terrace
          at a time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Planting trees knowing full well that time will pass is to receive the most beautiful of
          lessons: that of perseverance. Immediate profit is only the emptiness of a fleeting age,
          a race with no substance and no joy that produces nothing but people stripped of
          meaning. At a time of climate breakdown and its foretold catastrophes, I chose to bet on
          slowness. I would rather sculpt the beauty of a landscape with the years, bring forth
          healthy food by first nourishing the earth, and cultivate harmony with this wild world
          with which I share the secret of life. That is exactly the philosophy I hold up like a
          mirror to those who choose, for the length of a stay, to withdraw from the world with
          us.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here, the solar panels were installed long before there was anyone to light. The
          macadamias were planted in the ground with the certainty that they wouldn't bear a
          single fruit for fifteen years. Sheep and hens took possession of the land to work the
          soil, years before there was even one guest table to feed. This is the work of a
          farmer's lifetime, the legacy of knowledge and experience patiently ripened.
        </p>
        <p>
          It wasn't until 2025, eighteen years after the very first strike of the pickaxe, that
          our three handcrafted cabins finally opened their doors. Clinging to the hillside, they
          were built with the same stubborn patience as everything else here: in complicity with
          time, rather than the urgency of a deadline.
        </p>
        <p>— The Founder, Wild on the Farm</p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <QuoteSection cite="— Pascal, Founder">
    "I didn't want to build a place that looked like it belonged on this mountain. I wanted to
    build one that actually did — and that meant letting the farm come first, guests second."
  </QuoteSection>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <div class="section-label">Continue Exploring</div>
    <h2>Discover what that patience built.</h2>
    <p>Eighteen years of farming, three cabins, and a kitchen that starts just steps from the door.</p>
    <div class="contact-list">
      <a href="/farm/">The Farm →</a>
      <a href="/lodge/">The Lodge →</a>
      <a href="/food/">The Food →</a>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</PageLayout>
